{
  "dom_id": "step-q105501",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/a8345270e511644cfb019099b907ecadff056f7efbd396e84d4313f3c3ab5817.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)-2g(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)-2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(3)={f}^{′}(3)-2{g}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2,4)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (0,4)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/09e4836acd54b814c1691c60003614d206fc2eee5cda6d7ad8d6222dd72d2d4f.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-2}{4-2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (0,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-0}{4-0}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(3)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & ={f}^{′}(3)-2{g}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
